hi there steve kaufman here again  today i'm going to talk about memory and  language learning  remember if you like these videos please  subscribe and if you like this  particular video you can always give it  a thumbs up if you want  so memory memory  you know the ability to remember things  or more than the ability to remember  things deliberately trying to remember  things in other words  forcing yourself to remember  memorization memory  is always sort of indicated as a major  factor in language learning success  and i certainly remember trying  remember  when i was trying to remember  cases in german and it didn't really  matter how often i read the declension  tables i couldn't remember the endings  at least i couldn't remember them for  very long  um so  over time i came to realize that  language learning is not about memory  now  many people  are committed to spaced repetition  systems whereby some algorithm which  presents words to you in a certain order  um  based on some scientific uh study on how  frequently and at what interval you need  to see them in order to have a better  chance of remembering them so a lot of  people do this there are systems like  memorize and  anki and others  uh there are people who invest in  mnemonics in other words they identify  words with  i don't know parts of a house or other  things that will help them remember  and i have never  really  done this  i did for a while trying to nail down  cases i had tried it again with russian  case endings or  you know conjugations of  verbs and romance languages  and  it always seemed as if i was learning  something while i was studying the table  and then later on it all evaporated  and so i have come to the conclusion  that at least in so far as i am  concerned  it doesn't work for me and i think  memory is overrated as a  an important skill  in language learning  and so if i think about the reasons why  um  first of all  there are so many words if we talk about  vocabulary it's my experience that in  order to  operate comfortably in a language  uh like now i want to understand these  podcasts from al jazeera or  uh  you know eventually hopefully uh you  know read books in  i don't know arabic or greek or whatever  i need so many words and even  getting into conversations with people  the native speaker uses  has a much larger vocabulary than i will  ever have  and so i need a lot of words so many  words that if i spend my time  deliberately trying to remember the  words i won't have time for  engaging with the language in a more  meaningful way  so even if i read something and this is  before we had online dictionaries and  link and i would read a reader in  japanese and it had a word list in  behind each chapter i would just review  the word list and go and read the text  occasionally referring back to the word  list and somehow worked my way through  the text and what was really happening  is that i was being exposed to the text  to the words i didn't know to the words  i knew  i was reading and all of that exposure  was what was enabling me to become more  and more accustomed to the language more  familiar with the language but i didn't  try to deliberately memorize that word  list  because i had found that to be a futile  task um  my memories of language  are more you know where was i when i was  listening to ipromescis posey in italian  while jogging  or  or you know cross-country skiing through  the woods listening to  you know uh  a russian novel i'd see deity or  whatever it might be  i remember  it's pleasant when we go back to  languages he learned before i still have  the font this is my t-shirt that i got  it was given to me by a friend  uh the master master and margarita  but so that i have these pleasant  memories of my previous involvement with  languages that i have learned  uh you know  and it's pleasant to do that it's like  rediscovering old friends i go back even  within the languages that i'm now  learning  uh arabic and persian and turkish i go  back to stories that i did a while ago  and i'm  rediscovering them and and  discovering you know words that have  appeared subsequently elsewhere and then  they show up there again and i kind of  firm up my sense of those words and it's  all a pleasant  uh experience of rediscovery so so that  i have learned that the key thing in  language learning is your exposure to  the language in meaningful contexts  listening and reading and eventually  speaking which is also should be  meaningful not talking about  the weather in vancouver but engaging in  meaningful conversations  uh  so that all of this engagement with the  language is how the brain gradually gets  used to the new language  and trying to rely on sort of deliberate  memorization  is not a very  you know effective  approach  and of course there's all kinds of  research  that shows i mean there's two approaches  to memorization one is  perhaps the crudest form is to try and  sit there and absorb a table conjugation  table declension table or or list of  words and there's all kinds of research  that shows that this kind of black block  learning  simply doesn't work a more sophisticated  approach  is to say that here's a bunch of things  that i have to learn  and um  using a spaced repetition system  depending on how well i think i know  them then i put these back uh you know a  couple of hours couple of weeks a couple  of months so that they reappear again  and i'm forced to try to remember them  before i've completely forgotten them  which in theory probably works  but in order to practice that you have  to spend a lot of time at it simply  because there are so many words  and  and i would rather spend my time  engaging with interesting material  rather than sitting there and  deliberately reviewing uh these words  relying on this  theory that somehow  um  you know the words will reappear just in  the right frequency i have found that  some words you remember some words you  don't  could be a whatever association or  something that makes certain words stick  and other words not stick and certain  words that you remember for a while and  then you forget and then you rediscover  them again  and as long as you are doing enough  reading and listening these words the  ones you once learned and forgot and  relearned they'll keep coming back at  you as you become familiar with the  language and if you are familiar with  the language eventually you'll start to  be able to better use the language yes  there are people who understand well and  don't speak well  don't speak as well as they would like  there is no quick solution there you  just have to keep doing it keep  listening and reading keep speaking and  fight your way through  the difficulties that you have  in finding the words you need in getting  that you know construction right just  keep doing it keep doing it occasionally  refer to the grammar uh  just to kind of refresh your memory but  just keep using the language and and  that's what what it is so that this sort  of deliberately how can i best memorize  and how can i memorize the greatest  number or in japan they have these books  of words you need to know for toeica and  i have found that in order to be  effective in the language in order to do  well on a toeic exam or any other exam  you just need such a broad-based  level of familiarity with the language  vocabulary that no deliberate strategy  of just learning these words  i is going to do it so there you have it  my take on memory and language learning  and one more thing if you uh would like  to receive my 10 email course on my tips  to language learning please click on the  link here in the video bye for now
 

